Title: "Overview"
Id: "6867c1e2-40e0-47dd-8532-5ff5dab99055"

Text: "Proposition-like top-level items are `Axiom:`, `Theorem:`, and `Conjecture:`.
All three share the same assertion shape.

They may have an optional `[\command]` heading, but the head section itself
takes no argument: a result's name lives in `Documented:` `called:` (as for
definitions) and renders as the card's title. Their `then:` section is required.
`given:` sections accept refined-capable declarations, so forms such as
`f is \(injective)::function` are valid where refined statements are allowed.

```mlg-fragment
Axiom:
given: A is \set
then: \identity.function:on{A} is? \function:on{A}:to{A}
Documented:
. called: \"Identity is a function\"
Id: \"23e4567e-e89b-42d3-a456-426614174000\"
```

Items in `given:` introduce available type and specification facts along with
declared symbols. Items in `where:` are local assumptions available while
checking `then:` and `iff:`.

If a theorem-like group has a command heading, that heading introduces a command
signature and participates in duplicate-signature and reference checks.

The checker validates syntax, references, declared symbols, and command
requirements. It does not prove that the conclusion follows from the
assumptions.

`Corollary:` and `Lemma:` are not part of the structural language; a result that
follows from another is written as an ordinary `Theorem:`."
Id: "588dbd4b-f741-44cf-bcec-c8476066590a"
